    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing on high-ceiling leverage, anchored by **Brice Turang** and **Josh Naylor**. **Brice Turang** earns our top score of 67.64, driven by a leadoff spot against Rocker that grants him maximum plate appearance potential and a healthy +10% boost for his high-traffic role. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** finds himself in a prime position to feast on Scherzer\u2019s 5.40 xERA; the combination of a favorable hitter\u2019s park and the opponent's fading efficiency gives **Josh Naylor** a 76.43 score, making him our cornerstone play for RBI and HR upside. **Mickey Moniak** (66.79) and **Jose Caballero** (64.72) also provide critical efficiency, with both benefiting significantly from dynamic platoon advantages that push them well into the \"Strong\" start tier.\n\nOur most difficult decision of the day centered on **Isaac Paredes**, whose 50.34 score narrowly missed the cut. While **Isaac Paredes** projected well enough to merit consideration, his negative platoon split against Thornton and a disadvantageous park factor ultimately made him the odd man out in favor of the higher-floor profiles of **Austin Riley** and **Cole Young**. We are keeping a close eye on **Seiya Suzuki** (52.47); although his score is lower than preferred, his presence in the #2 hole against Burns offers enough protection to keep him in the lineup, despite the risk associated with a tough SP skill matchup. \n\nRisks remain, primarily regarding **Trevor Story** and **Cole Young**, both of whom are relegated to the bottom of the order against Warren and Scherzer, respectively. While **Cole Young** maintains a slight edge due to his SB potential\u2014a crucial component of our current strategy\u2014any late-game defensive substitutions or pinch-hitting scenarios could jeopardize these marginal plays. We are currently playing it safe by leaving **Zach McKinstry** on the bench, as his sub-40 score against the elite xERA of Glasnow makes him a liability for our daily efficiency targets.",
